(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Electronics II (Analog Design)"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Electronics II (Analog Design)"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Electronics II (Analog Design): Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nState the circuit topology, the assumptions (ideal or real op-amp, region of operation) and check the results (saturation, bandwidth, slew rate, power). All numerical results below were computed with Python (NumPy/SciPy) and checked by hand.\n\n# Assignment 1: Feedback and Operational Amplifier Circuits (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Feedback theory (12 marks)** An amplifier has A = 10^5. (a) Find the closed-loop gain and (1 + Aβ) for β = 0.01, 0.1 and 1 [4]. (b) If A falls by 20 percent, find the closed-loop gain for β = 0.01 and the percentage change [3]. (c) The amplifier has a single pole with GBW = 1 MHz: find the closed-loop bandwidth for a gain of 100 and of 10 [3]. (d) Explain how feedback changes input and output resistance in the four topologies [2].\n\n**Q2. Non-inverting amplifier with a real op-amp (10 marks)** R_f = 99 kΩ, R_g = 1 kΩ, GBW = 1 MHz, A_0 = 10^5, slew rate 0.5 V/μs, ±12 V rails. (a) Find the ideal gain and the gain error due to the finite A_0 [3]. (b) Find the bandwidth [2]. (c) Find the largest 20 kHz sine amplitude without slew-rate distortion, and the full-power bandwidth for a 5 V peak output [3]. (d) What input amplitude saturates the output? [2]\n\n**Q3. Offset and bias errors (10 marks)** An inverting amplifier has R_in = 1 kΩ and R_f = 100 kΩ with an op-amp having V_os = 3 mV and I_B = 80 nA (both inputs equal). (a) Find the noise gain and the output offset due to V_os [3]. (b) Find the output error due to the bias current without compensation [3]. (c) Where would you place a compensating resistor and what value? What effect remains? [4]\n\n**Q4. Instrumentation amplifier (10 marks)** The classic three-op-amp instrumentation amplifier has two input buffers with a gain-setting resistor R_g between them and 25 kΩ feedback resistors, followed by a unity-gain difference amplifier. (a) Find the gain for R_g = 1 kΩ [2]. (b) Find R_g for a gain of 1001 [2]. (c) A strain-gauge bridge excited at 5 V changes by ΔR/R = 0.1 percent in one arm: find the bridge output (V_ex ΔR/(4R)) and R_g for an output of 1 V [4]. (d) Why is the instrumentation amplifier better than a single difference amplifier for this job? [2]\n\n**Q5. Integrators and differentiators (10 marks)** (a) An inverting integrator has R = 10 kΩ and C = 10 nF. Find v_o for a 1 V step at t = 0 and the time to saturate at -12 V [4]. (b) Find its unity-gain frequency [2]. (c) Why does a practical integrator include a resistor in parallel with C? [2] (d) What does a differentiator do to a triangular wave and why is it noise-sensitive? [2]\n\n**Q6. Comparators and the Schmitt trigger (10 marks)** An inverting Schmitt trigger uses R_1 = 10 kΩ (to ground) and R_2 = 90 kΩ (positive feedback) with ±12 V saturation. (a) Find the thresholds and the hysteresis [4]. (b) Describe the output for a sine input of 3 V peak [3]. (c) Why does hysteresis help with a noisy input? [3]\n\n**Q7. Other circuits (8 marks)** (a) A transimpedance amplifier has R_f = 1 MΩ: find the output for a 2 μA photodiode current and the corner frequency with C_f = 1 pF [4]. (b) Show why a precision half-wave rectifier avoids the 0.7 V diode drop [2]. (c) What is the CMRR limit of a difference amplifier with 1 percent resistors and a gain of 10? [2]\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) A_f = A/(1 + Aβ) with A = 10^5:\nβ = 0.01: Aβ = 1000, 1 + Aβ = **1001**, A_f = 10^5/1001 = **99.9**.\nβ = 0.1: Aβ = 10^4, 1 + Aβ = **10,001**, A_f = **9.999**.\nβ = 1: Aβ = 10^5, 1 + Aβ = **100,001**, A_f = **0.99999** (a follower).\nIn each case A_f ≈ 1/β.\n(b) Step 1: A = 0.8 × 10^5 = 80,000: A_f = 80,000/(1 + 800) = **99.875**.\nStep 2: change = (99.875 - 99.900)/99.900 = **-0.025 percent** for a 20 percent change in A: the desensitivity factor is 1/(1 + Aβ) = 1/1001 (20 percent/1001 = 0.02 percent).\n(c) GBW = A_f × BW, so BW = GBW/A_f: gain 100: 1 MHz/100 = **10 kHz**; gain 10: 1 MHz/10 = **100 kHz**.\n(d) **Series mixing** at the input (feedback voltage in series with the input) **raises R_in** by (1 + Aβ); **shunt mixing** (feedback current at the input node) **lowers R_in**. **Voltage sampling** at the output **lowers R_out** by (1 + Aβ); **current sampling raises R_out**. So series-shunt (non-inverting amplifier): high R_in, low R_out; shunt-shunt (inverting): low R_in, low R_out; series-series: high/high; shunt-series: low/high.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Step 1: ideal gain = 1 + R_f/R_g = 1 + 99/1 = **100**.\nStep 2: β = R_g/(R_f + R_g) = 0.01, so the loop gain Aβ = 10^5 × 0.01 = 1000.\nStep 3: exact closed-loop gain = A/(1 + Aβ) = 10^5/1001 = 99.9: a **gain error of 0.1 percent**.\n(b) BW = GBW/gain = 1 MHz/100 = **10 kHz**.\n(c) Step 1: slew rate limit: 2π f V_p ≤ 0.5 V/μs, so V_p ≤ 0.5 × 10^6/(2π × 20,000) = **3.98 V** peak at 20 kHz.\nStep 2: full-power bandwidth for a 5 V peak output: f_max = SR/(2π V_p) = 0.5 × 10^6/(2π × 5) = **15.9 kHz** (the small-signal bandwidth of 10 kHz is lower: here the gain-bandwidth limit applies first).\n(d) The output saturates at ±12 V: the input amplitude = 12/100 = **0.12 V**.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: the noise gain for the offset = 1 + R_f/R_in = 1 + 100 = **101**.\nStep 2: output offset = 101 × 3 mV = **0.303 V** (about 0.3 V: far larger than the 1 mV of a precision application; use a low-offset op-amp or an adjustment).\n(b) The bias current of the inverting input flows through R_f (the inverting node is at virtual ground): v_o error = I_B R_f = 80 nA × 100 kΩ = **8 mV** (without a compensation resistor).\n(c) Add a resistor **R_3 = R_in ∥ R_f = 1 k ∥ 100 k = 990 Ω** between the non-inverting input and ground: both inputs then see the same resistance, so the equal bias currents produce equal voltage drops that cancel (the common-mode part). The error left is proportional to the **offset current** I_os = |I_B+ - I_B-| (typically 10 to 20 percent of I_B), for example 10 nA × 100 kΩ = 1 mV, plus the input offset voltage which is not removed by R_3.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Gain = 1 + 2R/R_g = 1 + 50 kΩ/1 kΩ = 1 + 50 = **51**.\n(b) 1001 = 1 + 50 kΩ/R_g → R_g = 50 kΩ/1000 = **50 Ω**.\n(c) Step 1: bridge output = V_ex ΔR/(4R) = 5 V × 0.001/4 = **1.25 mV**.\nStep 2: for 1 V output the gain is 1 V/1.25 mV = 800.\nStep 3: 1 + 50 kΩ/R_g = 800, so R_g = 50 kΩ/799 = **62.6 Ω** (62 Ω or a trimmer).\n(d) The input buffers give **very high and equal input resistances**, so the source impedance (the bridge) does not unbalance the circuit, and the gain is set by a **single resistor** without matching pairs; the common-mode rejection is high (80 to 120 dB) because the first stage amplifies only the difference signal while passing the common-mode signal with unity gain: a single difference amplifier has a low input resistance and its CMRR is limited by the match of four resistors.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Step 1: RC = 10 kΩ × 10 nF = 10^-4 s.\nStep 2: v_o = -(1/RC) ∫ v_in dt = -(1/10^-4) × 1 V × t = **-10,000 t volts** (-10 V/ms).\nStep 3: it reaches -12 V at t = 12/10,000 = **1.2 ms** and stays saturated.\n(b) f_unity = 1/(2π R C) = 1/(2π × 10^4 × 10^-8) = 1/(6.283 × 10^-4) = **1.59 kHz**.\n(c) With the capacitor alone, the DC gain is infinite: the input offset voltage and the bias current integrate until the output saturates. A resistor in parallel with C (R_p ≫ R) limits the DC gain to R_p/R and lets the output settle; the circuit then integrates only above 1/(2π R_p C).\n(d) The differentiator's output is -RC times the slope: a **triangular wave becomes a square wave**. Its gain rises at 20 dB/decade with frequency, so it amplifies noise and can oscillate with the op-amp's phase lag: practical designs add a series input resistor and a feedback capacitor to limit the high-frequency gain.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Step 1: β = R_1/(R_1 + R_2) = 10/100 = 0.1.\nStep 2: thresholds ±β V_sat = **±0.1 × 12 V = ±1.2 V**.\nStep 3: hysteresis = 2 × 1.2 = **2.4 V**.\n(b) The input is a 3 V peak sine. When the input rises above +1.2 V the output switches to -12 V; the output stays at -12 V until the input falls below -1.2 V, then switches to +12 V. The output is a **square wave of ±12 V** with the same frequency as the input and inverted (the switching instants occur when the sine crosses ±1.2 V, slightly after the zero crossings).\n(c) Noise of less than 2.4 V peak-to-peak riding on a slow signal that crosses a threshold cannot cause repeated switching, because after a transition the threshold moves to the other side by 2.4 V: the output makes one clean transition per crossing (a comparator with a single threshold would chatter).\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Step 1: v_o = -I_ph R_f = -2 μA × 1 MΩ = **-2 V**.\nStep 2: the feedback pole f = 1/(2π R_f C_f) = 1/(2π × 10^6 × 10^-12) = **159 kHz**. (The feedback capacitor also stabilises the amplifier against the photodiode's capacitance.)\n(b) In the precision half-wave rectifier the diode is inside the feedback loop of the op-amp. For a positive input the op-amp output rises to v_in + 0.7 V so that the diode conducts and the signal at the diode's cathode (the circuit output) equals v_in: the loop gain removes the 0.7 V drop (the effective drop is about 0.7 V divided by the open-loop gain). For a negative input the diode is off and the output stays at zero. The circuit therefore rectifies signals of only a few millivolts, which a simple diode circuit cannot.\n(c) Step 1: with resistor tolerance t = 1 percent, the worst-case CMRR ≈ (1 + G)/(4t).\nStep 2: = 11/0.04 = 275 = 20 log10(275) = **48.8 dB** (about 49 dB).\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 2: Active Filters, Oscillators and Timers (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Sallen-Key low-pass design (14 marks)** Design a unity-gain second-order Butterworth low-pass filter with f_0 = 1 kHz using R = 10 kΩ (H = 1/(s² R² C_1 C_2 + 2 s R C_2 + 1), f_0 = 1/(2π R sqrt(C_1 C_2)), Q = (1/2) sqrt(C_1/C_2)). (a) Find C_1 and C_2 for Q = 0.707 [5]. (b) With the standard values 22 nF and 10 nF, find the actual f_0 and Q [3]. (c) Find the attenuation at 2 kHz and 10 kHz for the ideal Butterworth response [4]. (d) What op-amp GBW is needed? [2]\n\n**Q2. Higher orders and types (8 marks)** (a) Find the Q values of the two stages of a fourth-order Butterworth filter and the slope of the roll-off [3]. (b) Describe a band-pass filter with f_0 = 1 kHz and Q = 10: find the bandwidth [2]. (c) Compare Butterworth, Chebyshev and Bessel responses [3].\n\n**Q3. Wien-bridge oscillator (10 marks)** R = 10 kΩ and C = 15.9 nF in the RC network. (a) Find the oscillation frequency [2]. (b) State the amplifier gain needed and design R_f and R_g [3]. (c) What happens if the gain is 2.9 or 3.2? [3] (d) Find the capacitor for a 340 Hz oscillator with the same resistors [2].\n\n**Q4. The 555 timer (14 marks)** (a) Astable: R_1 = 1 kΩ, R_2 = 10 kΩ, C = 0.1 μF. Find the frequency, the high time and the duty cycle [5]. (b) Monostable: R = 100 kΩ, C = 10 μF: find the pulse width [2]. (c) Design an astable for 1 kHz at 60 percent duty cycle with C = 0.1 μF [5]. (d) How could a duty cycle below 50 percent be obtained? [2]\n\n**Q5. Relaxation and LC oscillators (14 marks)** (a) An op-amp astable with R = 10 kΩ, C = 0.1 μF and the feedback ratio β = 0.5 has f = 1/(2RC ln((1 + β)/(1 - β))): find f; repeat for β = 0.1 [4]. (b) A Colpitts or Hartley oscillator has L = 10 μH and an effective capacitance of 100 pF: find f [2]. (c) State the Barkhausen criterion and how amplitude is stabilised [4]. (d) Why are crystals used in microcontroller clocks? [4]\n\n**Q6. PLL and VCO concept (10 marks)** Describe the blocks of a phase-locked loop, the lock condition, and give two applications.\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: Q = (1/2) sqrt(C_1/C_2) = 0.707 gives sqrt(C_1/C_2) = 1.414, so C_1 = 2 C_2.\nStep 2: f_0 = 1/(2π R sqrt(C_1 C_2)) = 1/(2π R sqrt(2) C_2), so C_2 = 1/(2π × 10^4 × 1000 × 1.414) = **11.25 nF**.\nStep 3: C_1 = 2 C_2 = **22.5 nF**.\n(b) Step 1: sqrt(22 nF × 10 nF) = sqrt(220) nF = 14.83 nF.\nStep 2: f_0 = 1/(2π × 10^4 × 14.83 nF) = **1073 Hz**.\nStep 3: Q = 0.5 sqrt(22/10) = 0.5 × 1.483 = **0.742** (the response is slightly peaked; the 5 percent error in f_0 from the standard values is acceptable; use E24 parts of 22 nF and 11 nF in practice, or paralleled capacitors).\n(c) Butterworth: |H| = 1/sqrt(1 + (f/f_0)^4).\nStep 1: at 2 kHz: 1/sqrt(1 + 16) = 1/sqrt 17 = 0.2425 = **-12.3 dB**.\nStep 2: at 10 kHz: 1/sqrt(10^4 + 1) = 0.01 = **-40 dB** (40 dB per decade).\n(d) The op-amp (a follower) must have a gain much larger than 1 at the highest frequency of interest: **GBW ≥ 20 to 100 × f_0 = 20 to 100 kHz** at least, and the slew rate must support the signal amplitude; a 1 MHz GBW op-amp is ample.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) A fourth-order Butterworth filter has two second-order sections with **Q = 0.541 and Q = 1.307**; the roll-off is **80 dB per decade** (24 dB per octave).\n(b) Q = f_0/BW, so BW = 1000/10 = **100 Hz** (a narrow-band filter; use a multiple-feedback or state-variable biquad).\n(c) **Butterworth:** the flattest passband, a moderate roll-off, some overshoot in the step response. **Chebyshev:** a steeper roll-off for a given order but with ripple in the passband (and more ringing). **Bessel:** the flattest group delay (best pulse response, minimal overshoot) but the slowest roll-off.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) f_0 = 1/(2π RC) = 1/(2π × 10^4 × 15.9 × 10^-9) = 1/(9.99 × 10^-4) = **1001 Hz**.\n(b) The RC network attenuates the signal by 3 at f_0 with zero phase, so the amplifier needs a gain of **3** (a non-inverting amplifier with 1 + R_f/R_g = 3, i.e. **R_f = 2 R_g**, for example R_g = 10 kΩ and R_f = 20 kΩ), slightly more than 3 to start the oscillation.\n(c) With a gain of **2.9** (loop gain < 1) any oscillation **dies out**; with **3.2** (loop gain > 1) the amplitude grows until the op-amp clips (saturates), producing a distorted waveform. A practical circuit uses an amplitude-control element (a lamp, diodes or an FET) so that the gain falls as the amplitude rises, reaching exactly 3.\n(d) C = 1/(2π R f) = 1/(2π × 10^4 × 340) = 1/(2.136 × 10^7) = **46.8 nF** (use 47 nF: 338.6 Hz).\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: f = 1.44/((R_1 + 2R_2) C) = 1.44/((1 k + 20 k) × 0.1 μF) = 1.44/(2.1 × 10^-3) = **685.7 Hz**.\nStep 2: high time = 0.693 (R_1 + R_2) C = 0.693 × 11 kΩ × 0.1 μF = **0.762 ms**.\nStep 3: low time = 0.693 R_2 C = 0.693 ms; period = 1.455 ms (685.7 Hz ✓).\nStep 4: duty cycle = (R_1 + R_2)/(R_1 + 2R_2) = 11/21 = **52.4 percent**.\n(b) T = 1.1 R C = 1.1 × 100 kΩ × 10 μF = **1.1 s**.\n(c) Step 1: duty = (R_1 + R_2)/(R_1 + 2R_2) = 0.6 gives R_1 + R_2 = 0.6 R_1 + 1.2 R_2, so 0.4 R_1 = 0.2 R_2 and **R_2 = 2 R_1**.\nStep 2: f = 1.44/((R_1 + 2R_2) C) = 1.44/(5 R_1 C) = 1000 Hz, so 5 R_1 = 1.44/(1000 × 10^-7) = 14.4 kΩ.\nStep 3: **R_1 = 2.88 kΩ and R_2 = 5.76 kΩ** (use 2.87 kΩ and 5.76 kΩ, 1 percent values).\n(d) Place a **diode in parallel with R_2** (anode towards the capacitor-discharge pin) so that charging bypasses R_2: the high time becomes 0.693 R_1 C and the low time 0.693 R_2 C, so the duty can be below 50 percent if R_1 < R_2.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Step 1: β = 0.5: (1 + β)/(1 - β) = 3, ln 3 = 1.0986: f = 1/(2 × 10^4 × 10^-7 × 1.0986) = 1/(2.197 × 10^-3) = **455 Hz**.\nStep 2: β = 0.1: ln(1.1/0.9) = 0.2007: f = 1/(2 × 10^-3 × 0.2007) = **2.49 kHz**. (Hysteresis sets the swing of the capacitor voltage and therefore the frequency.)\n(b) f = 1/(2π sqrt(LC)) = 1/(2π sqrt(10^-5 × 10^-10)) = 1/(2π × 3.16 × 10^-8) = **5.03 MHz**.\n(c) **Barkhausen:** the loop gain A β must have magnitude at least 1 (exactly 1 in steady state) and the total loop phase shift must be 0° (a multiple of 360°) at the oscillation frequency. Start-up needs |Aβ| slightly above 1; the amplitude is stabilised by a **non-linearity** (clipping or a gain-control element such as a lamp, thermistor or diode network) that reduces the gain as the amplitude grows until |Aβ| = 1.\n(d) A **quartz crystal** has a very high Q (10^4 to 10^6) and a frequency stable to 10 to 50 ppm, vastly better than RC or LC oscillators (about 1 to 5 percent), a necessity for accurate baud rates, timers, USB and radio; it is inexpensive and small.\n\n**Q6.** A **PLL** has a **phase detector** (compares the phase of the input with that of the VCO output), a **loop (low-pass) filter** (smooths the error to a control voltage) and a **voltage-controlled oscillator** (whose frequency depends on the control voltage), with an optional divider in the feedback path. The loop **locks** when the VCO frequency equals the input frequency (or N times it with a divider) and the phase error is constant (zero or a fixed offset): the control voltage then holds the VCO at the right frequency. Applications: **frequency synthesis** (a stable crystal reference multiplied to the GHz clock of a processor or a radio local oscillator), **clock recovery and clean-up**, **FM demodulation**, motor speed control, carrier recovery in receivers.\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 3: Stability, Power Stages, Regulators and Integrated-Circuit Building Blocks (80 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Phase margin (14 marks)** An op-amp has A_0 = 10^5 with a dominant pole at 10 Hz and a second pole at f_2. (a) Find the unity-gain (crossover) frequency with β = 1 [2]. (b) Compute the phase margin for f_2 = 1, 2, 5 and 10 MHz [6]. (c) Which values are acceptable? Describe the closed-loop step response for PM = 45° and PM = 80° [4]. (d) What is the effect of a capacitive load on stability and the remedy? [2]\n\n**Q2. Compensation (8 marks)** Explain pole splitting and Miller compensation, with a numerical example: a second-stage gain of -100 and C_c = 10 pF.\n\n**Q3. Output stages (18 marks)** A class B push-pull stage uses ±12 V supplies and an 8 Ω load. (a) Find the output power, supply power and efficiency at a peak output of 12 V [4]. (b) Find the maximum device dissipation (total) and the output voltage at which it occurs [4]. (c) Explain crossover distortion and how class AB solves it [4]. (d) Design a class AB stage for 20 W into 8 Ω: find V_p, the peak current, the supply voltage (add a 4 V margin), supply power and efficiency [6]\n\n**Q4. Linear regulators and thermal design (10 marks)** A 7805 regulator supplies 5 V at 0.5 A from 12 V. (a) Find the power dissipation and the efficiency [3]. (b) If θ_JA = 65 °C/W without a heat sink, find the junction temperature at 25 °C ambient and comment [3]. (c) With a heat sink: θ_JC = 5, θ_CS = 1, θ_SA = 8 °C/W at 3.5 W: find T_J at 40 °C ambient [4]\n\n**Q5. Switching regulator (10 marks)** A buck converter converts 12 V to 5 V with L = 47 μH at 100 kHz. (a) Find the duty cycle [2]. (b) Find the inductor ripple current [3]. (c) Find the output ripple for C = 100 μF [3]. (d) Compare the efficiency with a linear regulator at 0.5 A (assume the buck is 90 percent efficient) [2]\n\n**Q6. Differential pair and current mirrors (10 marks)** (a) A BJT differential pair has a tail current of 1 mA and R_C = 10 kΩ (single-ended output): find g_m and the differential gain [4]. (b) A simple BJT current mirror with β = 100 has I_ref = 1 mA: find the output current error from the base currents [3]. (c) Why do op-amps use active loads? [3]\n\n**Q7. Noise and precision (10 marks)** (a) Find the thermal noise voltage of 1 kΩ and 100 kΩ resistors over a 1 kHz bandwidth at 300 K [4]. (b) Find the SNR in dB of a 1 V rms signal and 10 μV rms noise [2]. (c) List four design measures to reduce noise and interference [4]\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) The first pole is at 10 Hz, so |A| = 10^5/sqrt(1 + (f/10)²) ≈ 10^6/f: unity at about **1 MHz** (GBW = 10^5 × 10 Hz = 1 MHz), slightly lower with the second pole.\n(b) PM = 180° - arctan(f/10) - arctan(f/f_2) at f = 1 MHz; the first arctan is 90° (practically 89.99°).\nStep 1: f_2 = 1 MHz: 180 - 90 - 45 = **45°**.\nStep 2: f_2 = 2 MHz: 180 - 90 - arctan 0.5 = 180 - 90 - 26.6 = **63.4°**.\nStep 3: f_2 = 5 MHz: 180 - 90 - 11.3 = **78.7°**.\nStep 4: f_2 = 10 MHz: 180 - 90 - 5.7 = **84.3°**.\n(c) PM ≥ 45° to 60° is acceptable; **45°** gives a step response with about 20 to 25 percent overshoot and some ringing (the closed-loop peaking); **63°** gives 5 to 10 percent; **80°** is nearly critically damped (about 1 percent overshoot) but a slower response.\n(d) A capacitive load with the op-amp's output resistance adds a pole (a phase lag) inside the loop, reducing the phase margin and causing ringing. Remedies: an **isolation resistor** (10 to 100 Ω) in series with the output and a feedback capacitor/resistor around it, a lower closed-loop bandwidth, or an op-amp designed for capacitive loads.\n\n**Q2.** Step 1: A two-stage amplifier without compensation has two poles close in frequency, giving a phase margin near zero for unity feedback.\nStep 2: **Pole splitting:** adding a capacitor C_c across the second gain stage (between its input and output) produces the Miller effect: the effective capacitance at the first stage's output is C_c (1 + |A_2|) = 10 pF × 101 = **1010 pF**, which moves the first (dominant) pole to a much lower frequency, while the feedback at the second stage's output **pushes the non-dominant pole to a much higher frequency** (the poles split apart).\nStep 3: The result is a single dominant pole with the second pole well above the crossover frequency, so the PM exceeds 60°.\nStep 4: The price is a lower GBW (g_m1/(2π C_c)) and a limited slew rate (I/C_c).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: V_p = 12 V: P_out = V_p²/(2R_L) = 144/16 = **9 W**.\nStep 2: P_supply = 2 V_cc V_p/(π R_L) = 2 × 12 × 12/(π × 8) = 288/25.13 = **11.46 W**.\nStep 3: efficiency = 9/11.46 = **78.5 percent** (π/4).\n(b) The total dissipation P_D = P_supply - P_out = (2 V_cc V_p)/(π R_L) - V_p²/(2R_L) is a maximum at V_p = 2 V_cc/π = **7.64 V** (about 64 percent of V_cc): **P_D,max = 2 V_cc²/(π² R_L) = 2 × 144/(9.87 × 8) = 3.65 W** (1.8 W per transistor), which is less than at full output (2.46 W) but is the worst case for the heat sink design (music signals have a high crest factor).\n(c) A class B stage has a dead zone: until the input exceeds about ±0.6 to 0.7 V, neither transistor conducts, so the output has a flat spot at each zero crossing (**crossover distortion**), most visible at low signal levels. **Class AB:** two diodes (or a V_BE multiplier) between the bases bias both transistors just on (a quiescent current of a few mA to tens of mA), so one takes over smoothly from the other and the dead zone disappears; the cost is a small quiescent dissipation. Add negative feedback around the amplifier to reduce the distortion further.\n(d) Step 1: V_p = sqrt(2 P R_L) = sqrt(2 × 20 × 8) = sqrt 320 = **17.9 V**.\nStep 2: I_p = V_p/R_L = 17.9/8 = **2.24 A**.\nStep 3: V_cc = 17.9 + 4 V = about **22 V** (±22 V supplies).\nStep 4: P_supply = 2 V_cc V_p/(π R_L) = 2 × 22 × 17.9/(π × 8) = **31.3 W**.\nStep 5: efficiency = 20/31.3 = **63.9 percent**; the total dissipation in the transistors = 31.3 - 20 = 11.3 W at full power (the heat sinks must handle the worst case 2 V_cc²/(π² R_L) = 12.3 W at V_p = 14 V).\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: P = (V_in - V_out) I = (12 - 5) × 0.5 = **3.5 W**.\nStep 2: efficiency = P_out/P_in = 2.5 W/6 W = 5/12 = **41.7 percent**.\n(b) T_J = T_A + P θ_JA = 25 + 3.5 × 65 = **252 °C**: far above the 125 to 150 °C limit; the regulator would shut down by thermal protection. A heat sink is needed (or a lower input voltage or a switching regulator).\n(c) Step 1: θ_JA(total) = θ_JC + θ_CS + θ_SA = 5 + 1 + 8 = 14 °C/W.\nStep 2: T_J = 40 + 3.5 × 14 = **89 °C**: acceptable.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) D = V_out/V_in = 5/12 = **0.417**.\n(b) Step 1: ΔI_L = (V_in - V_out) D/(L f) = 7 × 0.417/(47 × 10^-6 × 10^5) = 2.92/4.7 = **0.62 A peak-to-peak**.\nStep 2: with an average current of 0.5 A the peak is 0.5 + 0.31 = 0.81 A and the valley 0.19 A: still continuous conduction.\n(c) ΔV_o = ΔI_L/(8 f C) = 0.62/(8 × 10^5 × 100 × 10^-6) = 0.62/80 = **7.8 mV** (ignoring the capacitor's ESR, which often dominates in real designs).\n(d) Step 1: input power for the buck = 2.5 W/0.9 = 2.78 W, loss 0.28 W.\nStep 2: the linear regulator draws 12 V × 0.5 A = 6 W, loss 3.5 W.\nStep 3: the buck wastes 3.5/0.28 = **12 times less**, at the cost of complexity and switching noise.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Step 1: each transistor carries 0.5 mA: g_m = 0.5 mA/25 mV = **20 mS**.\nStep 2: the single-ended output differential gain A_d = g_m R_C = 20 mS × 10 kΩ = **200** (46 dB) (the differential output would be 400).\n(b) Step 1: the mirror reference current I_ref supplies the collector current and the two base currents: I_ref = I_C + 2 I_C/β.\nStep 2: I_out = I_ref/(1 + 2/β) = 1 mA/1.02 = **0.980 mA** (a 2 percent error).\n(c) A current-mirror **active load** presents a very high small-signal resistance (r_o) while needing only a small DC voltage, so a single stage obtains a gain of g_m r_o (hundreds), and it converts the differential output to single-ended without losing half the signal; it saves chip area (no large resistors).\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) v_n = sqrt(4 k T R Δf):\nStep 1: 1 kΩ: sqrt(4 × 1.38 × 10^-23 × 300 × 1000 × 1000) = sqrt(1.656 × 10^-14) = **0.129 μV** rms.\nStep 2: 100 kΩ: 0.129 × sqrt 100 = **1.29 μV** rms (a factor of 10 for a factor of 100 in R).\n(b) SNR = 20 log10(1 V/10 μV) = 20 log10(10^5) = 20 × 5 = **100 dB**.\n(c) Use **lower resistor values** where possible (less thermal noise), **limit the bandwidth** to what the signal needs, choose **low-noise devices** (low e_n and i_n), use **shielding and twisted pairs**, separate analog and digital grounds and **decouple** supplies, put the **gain early** in the signal chain, and use differential signalling and instrumentation amplifiers for low-level signals.\n","exam":"# Electronics II (Analog Design): Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks. Assume ideal op-amps unless stated.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** The closed-loop gain of a feedback amplifier is\nA) A(1 + Aβ)  B) A/(1 + Aβ)  C) Aβ  D) A - β\n\n**2.** Series mixing with shunt sampling at the output (series-shunt feedback) gives\nA) low R_in and high R_out  B) high R_in and low R_out  C) low R_in and low R_out  D) high R_in and high R_out\n\n**3.** The noise gain of a non-inverting amplifier with R_f = 99 kΩ and R_g = 1 kΩ is\nA) 1  B) 99  C) 100  D) 0.01\n\n**4.** A 1 MHz GBW op-amp with a gain of 50 has a small-signal bandwidth of\nA) 20 kHz  B) 50 kHz  C) 1 MHz  D) 50 MHz\n\n**5.** Slew-rate limiting distorts\nA) small signals at low frequencies  B) large, high-frequency signals  C) DC signals  D) only square waves of low amplitude\n\n**6.** The bias current compensation resistor in an inverting amplifier equals\nA) R_f  B) R_in  C) R_in ∥ R_f  D) 0\n\n**7.** An op-amp comparator without feedback\nA) has a virtual short  B) saturates according to the sign of v+ - v-  C) amplifies linearly  D) oscillates\n\n**8.** The thresholds of an inverting Schmitt trigger depend on\nA) the supply only  B) the saturation voltage and the feedback ratio  C) the input frequency  D) the load\n\n**9.** A unity-gain Sallen-Key low-pass with equal resistors has Q = 0.707 when\nA) C_1 = C_2  B) C_1 = 2 C_2  C) C_1 = C_2/2  D) R_1 = 2 R_2\n\n**10.** Each additional pole in a low-pass filter adds roll-off of\nA) 6 dB/octave  B) 20 dB/decade  C) both of the above (they are equal)  D) 60 dB/decade\n\n**11.** The Barkhausen criterion requires, at the oscillation frequency, a loop phase shift of\nA) 90°  B) 180°  C) 0° or 360°  D) 45°\n\n**12.** The 555 astable frequency is\nA) 1.1/(RC)  B) 1.44/((R_1 + 2R_2) C)  C) 0.693 RC  D) 1/(2π RC)\n\n**13.** A crystal oscillator is preferred in a microcontroller because of its\nA) low cost only  B) high Q and frequency stability  C) large size  D) wide tolerance\n\n**14.** The phase margin is the phase of the loop gain relative to -180° at the frequency where\nA) |Aβ| = 1  B) the phase is zero  C) |Aβ| = 0  D) the gain is maximum\n\n**15.** Miller compensation makes the amplifier stable by\nA) adding a zero  B) creating a dominant low-frequency pole and pushing the second pole higher  C) removing feedback  D) increasing the supply\n\n**16.** In class AB output stages the quiescent current is set to\nA) zero  B) a small value that removes the crossover distortion  C) the maximum rating  D) the load current\n\n**17.** The maximum efficiency of a class A stage with a resistive load is\nA) 25 percent  B) 50 percent  C) 78.5 percent  D) 100 percent\n\n**18.** Compared with a linear regulator for a large V_in - V_out, a switching regulator has\nA) lower efficiency  B) higher efficiency but more noise  C) lower cost always  D) no ripple\n\n**19.** In a buck converter V_out = D V_in. For V_in = 15 V and V_out = 5 V the duty cycle is\nA) 0.25  B) 0.33  C) 0.5  D) 0.75\n\n**20.** Thermal (Johnson) noise voltage increases with\nA) the square root of resistance and bandwidth  B) 1/R  C) frequency only  D) the supply voltage\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** A non-inverting amplifier has R_f = 20 kΩ and R_g = 1 kΩ. The op-amp has an input offset voltage of 2 mV. Find the ideal gain and the output offset error. If the GBW is 1 MHz, find the bandwidth.\n\n**B2. (5)** A unity-gain Sallen-Key low-pass filter has R = 16 kΩ, C_1 = 20 nF and C_2 = 10 nF. Find f_0 and Q.\n\n**B3. (5)** A 555 monostable uses R = 47 kΩ and C = 22 μF. Find the pulse width. What value of R gives 10 s with the same C?\n\n**B4. (5)** An op-amp has a slew rate of 1 V/μs. Find the full-power bandwidth for a 5 V peak output. What peak amplitude can it deliver at 100 kHz?\n\n**B5. (5)** A feedback amplifier has a crossover frequency of 1 MHz where the first pole contributes -90° and a second pole at 2.5 MHz. Find the phase margin and comment.\n\n**B6. (5)** A class AB stage with ±15 V supplies delivers 12 V peak into 8 Ω. Estimate the efficiency (use the class B formula) and the power in the load.\n\n**B7. (5)** A buck converter converts 12 V to 3.3 V at 500 kHz with L = 22 μH. Find the duty cycle and the inductor ripple current.\n\n**B8. (5)** A current mirror has a 1:3 area ratio and a reference current of 0.5 mA. Find the output current (ignore base currents), and the error if β = 100 and the base currents are included for a simple two-transistor mirror with 1:1 ratio.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. Sensor amplifier.** A type-K thermocouple produces 41 μV/°C. The temperature range 0 to 100 °C (0 to 4.1 mV) must be amplified to 0 to 5 V for an ADC.\n(a) Find the required gain and design an instrumentation amplifier stage (R = 25 kΩ feedback resistors) giving it [4]. (b) The op-amp has a 50 μV input offset: find the equivalent temperature error [2]. (c) Why is an instrumentation amplifier chosen and which op-amp specifications matter (offset, drift, CMRR, noise)? [4]\n\n**C2. Anti-aliasing filter.** An ADC samples at 20 kHz and the signal band is 0 to 5 kHz.\n(a) Design a second-order unity-gain Sallen-Key Butterworth filter with f_c = 5 kHz using R = 10 kΩ (give C_1 and C_2) [4]. (b) Find the attenuation at 15 kHz (the lowest alias of interest, 20 - 5) for second- and fourth-order Butterworth filters [3]. (c) Choose the minimum GBW of the op-amp [2]. (d) Which filter response would you choose for pulse signals? [1]\n\n**C3. Oscillator design.** Design a 10 kHz Wien-bridge oscillator.\n(a) Choose R and C (use C = 10 nF) [2]. (b) Find the gain needed and R_f, R_g, and explain the start-up condition [3]. (c) Describe how the amplitude is stabilised [2]. (d) State the op-amp GBW and slew-rate requirements for a 5 V peak output [3].\n\n**C4. Audio power amplifier.** Design a class AB amplifier for 10 W into 4 Ω with ±12 V supplies.\n(a) Find V_p and the peak current [2]. (b) Find the supply power and the efficiency at full output [3]. (c) Find the worst-case total dissipation and the heat sink requirement for each of two transistors (T_J ≤ 125 °C at 25 °C ambient: the case-to-sink and junction-to-case resistances are 0.5 and 1.5 °C/W) [4]. (d) Explain crossover distortion [1].\n\n**C5. Stability analysis.** A feedback amplifier has the loop gain Aβ = 1000/((1 + s/ω_1)(1 + s/ω_2)(1 + s/ω_3)) with poles at 100 Hz, 1 MHz and 5 MHz.\n(a) Estimate the crossover frequency and the phase margin [4]. (b) Find the gain margin using the frequency 2.24 MHz at which the phase is -180° (|Aβ| = 0.0167 there) [3]. (c) Describe the step response and what happens if the loop gain is doubled [3].\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **B** · 2 **B** · 3 **C** · 4 **A** · 5 **B** · 6 **C** · 7 **B** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **C** · 11 **C** · 12 **B** · 13 **B** · 14 **A** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **A** · 18 **B** · 19 **B** · 20 **A**\n\nBrief reasons: 3. Noise gain = 1 + R_f/R_g = 100. 4. 1 MHz/50 = 20 kHz. 9. Q = (1/2) sqrt(C_1/C_2). 19. 5/15 = 0.33.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** Step 1: ideal gain = 1 + R_f/R_g = 1 + 20/1 = **21**.\nStep 2: output offset = 21 × 2 mV = **42 mV**.\nStep 3: bandwidth = GBW/gain = 1 MHz/21 = **47.6 kHz**.\n\n**B2.** Step 1: sqrt(C_1 C_2) = sqrt(20 nF × 10 nF) = 14.14 nF.\nStep 2: f_0 = 1/(2π R sqrt(C_1 C_2)) = 1/(2π × 16,000 × 14.14 nF) = 1/(1.421 × 10^-3) = **703 Hz**.\nStep 3: Q = (1/2) sqrt(C_1/C_2) = (1/2) sqrt 2 = **0.707** (Butterworth).\n\n**B3.** Step 1: T = 1.1 R C = 1.1 × 47 kΩ × 22 μF = 1.1 × 1.034 = **1.14 s**.\nStep 2: for 10 s: R = T/(1.1 C) = 10/(1.1 × 22 μF) = 10/(2.42 × 10^-5) = **413 kΩ** (use 390 kΩ in series with a 22 kΩ trimmer).\n\n**B4.** Step 1: f_max = SR/(2π V_p) = 10^6/(2π × 5) = **31.8 kHz**.\nStep 2: at 100 kHz: V_p = SR/(2π f) = 10^6/(2π × 10^5) = **1.59 V** peak.\n\n**B5.** Step 1: PM = 180° - 90° - arctan(f_c/f_2) = 90° - arctan(1/2.5).\nStep 2: arctan 0.4 = 21.8°, so PM = 90° - 21.8° = **68.2°**: well above 45° to 60°, a stable well-damped amplifier with little overshoot (a few percent).\n\n**B6.** Step 1: P_out = V_p²/(2R_L) = 12²/(2 × 8) = **9 W**.\nStep 2: P_supply = 2 V_cc V_p/(π R_L) = 2 × 15 × 12/(π × 8) = 14.3 W.\nStep 3: efficiency = P_out/P_supply = (π/4)(V_p/V_cc) = 0.785 × 0.8 = **62.8 percent** (class AB has a slightly lower efficiency because of the quiescent current).\n\n**B7.** Step 1: D = V_out/V_in = 3.3/12 = **0.275**.\nStep 2: ΔI_L = (V_in - V_out) D/(L f) = 8.7 × 0.275/(22 μH × 500 kHz) = 2.39/11 = **0.217 A** peak-to-peak.\n\n**B8.** Step 1: with a 1:3 ratio: I_out = 3 × 0.5 mA = **1.5 mA**.\nStep 2: for the 1:1 simple mirror with base currents: I_out = I_ref/(1 + 2/β) = I_ref/1.02 = **0.98 I_ref**, an error of **2 percent**.\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a) Gain = 5 V/4.1 mV = **1220** (1219). An instrumentation amplifier with R = 25 kΩ: 1 + 2R/R_g = 1219, so R_g = 50 kΩ/1218 = **41 Ω** (use a 39 Ω resistor in series with a 5 Ω trimmer, or 41.2 Ω 0.1 percent); a unity-gain difference amplifier follows. (Often split into two stages to reduce the effect of the GBW and offsets.)\n(b) 50 μV/41 μV per °C = **1.2 °C** error (before the gain is applied the offset is equivalent to 1.2 °C).\n(c) The thermocouple signal is small (millivolts) and appears on top of a common-mode voltage from the environment, so the amplifier needs **high CMRR (over 100 dB)** and a **high input resistance**. The instrumentation amplifier's gain is set by one resistor and matched internal resistors give the CMRR. Critical specifications: **low offset and drift** (μV and μV/°C), **low noise** (nV/√Hz), high CMRR, adequate bandwidth, and input bias current (low for high-impedance sources). Add a cold-junction compensation measurement, an input RC filter against interference and a protection network.\n\n**C2.**\n(a) Step 1: Q = 0.707 requires C_1 = 2 C_2 and f_c = 1/(2π R sqrt 2 C_2).\nStep 2: C_2 = 1/(2π × 10^4 × 5000 × 1.414) = **2.25 nF** (2.2 nF) and C_1 = 2 C_2 = **4.5 nF** (4.7 nF), R = 10 kΩ.\n(b) |H(15 kHz)| = 1/sqrt(1 + (15/5)^(2n)):\nStep 1: n = 2: 1/sqrt(1 + 3^4) = 1/sqrt 82 = 0.110 = **-19.1 dB**.\nStep 2: n = 4: 1/sqrt(1 + 3^8) = 1/sqrt 6562 = 0.0123 = **-38.2 dB**.\n(c) GBW ≥ 100 × f_c = 100 × 5 kHz = **0.5 MHz** (use a 1 to 10 MHz op-amp); the slew rate must support the signal.\n(d) A **Bessel** filter (linear phase and no overshoot for pulses).\n\n**C3.**\n(a) f = 1/(2π RC) = 10 kHz with C = 10 nF: R = 1/(2π × 10^4 × 10^-8) = 1/(6.283 × 10^-4) = **1.59 kΩ** (use 1.58 kΩ, 1 percent).\n(b) The RC network attenuates by 3 at f_0, so the amplifier gain must be **3**: 1 + R_f/R_g = 3, **R_f = 2 R_g** (for example 3.16 kΩ and 1.58 kΩ). For start-up the gain must be slightly above 3 (for example 3.1) so that the loop gain exceeds 1 and the oscillation grows from noise.\n(c) An **amplitude-control** element reduces the gain as the output grows: a small incandescent lamp or a thermistor in the feedback path (the resistance rises with the signal power), or back-to-back diodes in parallel with a part of R_f (they conduct at larger amplitudes and lower the gain), settling where the loop gain is exactly 1.\n(d) The amplifier has a gain of 3 at 10 kHz: GBW ≥ 3 × 10 kHz × (a margin of 10 to 100) = **0.3 to 3 MHz** (so that the phase error of the op-amp does not shift the frequency noticeably). The slew rate: SR ≥ 2π f V_p = 2π × 10^4 × 5 = **0.31 V/μs** (use at least 1 V/μs).\n\n**C4.**\n(a) Step 1: V_p = sqrt(2 P R_L) = sqrt(2 × 10 × 4) = sqrt 80 = **8.94 V**.\nStep 2: I_p = V_p/R_L = 8.94/4 = **2.24 A**.\n(b) Step 1: P_supply = 2 V_cc V_p/(π R_L) = 2 × 12 × 8.94/(π × 4) = **17.1 W**.\nStep 2: efficiency = 10/17.1 = **58.5 percent**; total dissipation at full output = 17.1 - 10 = 7.1 W.\n(c) Step 1: the worst-case total dissipation (at V_p = 2V_cc/π = 7.64 V) = 2 V_cc²/(π² R_L) = 288/39.5 = **7.3 W**, so each transistor dissipates 3.65 W.\nStep 2: for T_J ≤ 125 °C at 25 °C ambient: θ_JA ≤ (125 - 25)/3.65 = 27.4 °C/W, so θ_SA ≤ 27.4 - 1.5 - 0.5 = **25.4 °C/W** (a small heat sink; with a 10 °C/W heat sink T_J = 25 + 3.65 × 12 = 69 °C for an easy margin).\n(d) In class B the transistors do not conduct until the input exceeds about ±0.7 V, so the output has a dead zone at each zero crossing; class AB biasing removes it.\n\n**C5.**\n(a) Find |Aβ| = 1: |Aβ| ≈ 1000/(f/100) at f ≫ 100 Hz and well below 1 MHz: 1000/(f/100) = 10^5/f = 1 gives f ≈ 100 kHz; including the second pole slightly lowers it to **99.5 kHz**. The phase there = -(arctan(995) + arctan(0.0995) + arctan(0.0199)) = -(89.94° + 5.68° + 1.14°) = -96.8°: **PM = 180 - 96.8 = 83.2°**.\n(b) The phase reaches -180° at about 2.24 MHz, where |Aβ| = 0.0167: **gain margin = 1/0.0167 = 60 = 35.6 dB**: the loop gain could be increased by a factor of 60 before the amplifier oscillates.\n(c) With PM = 83° the amplifier is nearly critically damped: the step response rises smoothly with practically **no overshoot (about 1 percent)** and a rise time of about 0.35/f_c = 3.5 μs (a closed-loop bandwidth of about 100 kHz). Doubling the loop gain moves the crossover to about 200 kHz (the phase there is about -(89.97° + 11.3° + 2.3°) = -103.6°): **PM falls to about 76°**, still very stable, and the gain margin falls to 29.6 dB (the amplifier remains stable until the gain is raised by 30 times).\n","notes":"# Electronics II (Analog Design): Complete Lecture Notes\n\nElectronics II builds on the devices and single-stage amplifiers of Electronics I to design real analog systems: feedback amplifiers, operational-amplifier circuits, active filters, oscillators, power amplifiers and regulators. The unifying theme is **negative feedback**: we build precise, stable circuits from imprecise, high-gain parts. Notation: A is the open-loop (basic) gain, β the feedback factor, Aβ the loop gain, f_T (or GBW) the gain-bandwidth product, V_T the thermal voltage.\n\n## Chapter 1. Negative Feedback\n\n### 1.1 The basic feedback loop\nA feedback amplifier has a forward gain A, a feedback network β, and a summing node: **A_f = A/(1 + Aβ)**. If the loop gain **Aβ ≫ 1** then **A_f ≈ 1/β**: the gain is set by passive components, not by the transistors. Example: A = 10^5 and β = 0.01 gives A_f = 99.9 (ideal 100); 1 + Aβ = 1001 is the **amount of feedback**.\n- **Gain desensitivity:** dA_f/A_f = (dA/A)/(1 + Aβ): a 10 percent change of A (from 10^5 to 0.9 × 10^5) changes A_f by only 0.01 percent.\n- **Bandwidth extension:** for a single-pole amplifier the closed-loop bandwidth is f_H(1 + Aβ), so **A_f × BW = constant = GBW**: a gain of 100 with a GBW of 1 MHz has a 10 kHz bandwidth.\n- **Distortion and noise** are reduced by (1 + Aβ); **input and output resistances** are modified depending on how the feedback is connected.\n- **Cost:** reduced gain and a risk of **instability** if the phase shift around the loop reaches 180° at a frequency where |Aβ| ≥ 1.\n\n### 1.2 The four feedback topologies\nThe topology is named by how the output is sampled and how the feedback is mixed at the input:\n- **Series-shunt** (voltage amplifier: sample voltage, mix voltage in series): increases R_in by (1 + Aβ), decreases R_out by (1 + Aβ): the non-inverting op-amp amplifier.\n- **Shunt-shunt** (transresistance amplifier): decreases both R_in and R_out: the inverting op-amp amplifier (virtual ground at the input).\n- **Series-series** (transconductance amplifier): increases R_in and R_out: a source-degenerated amplifier.\n- **Shunt-series** (current amplifier): decreases R_in, increases R_out.\nAnalysis method: identify the topology, break the loop to find A and β with the loading of the feedback network included, then apply the formulas.\n\n### 1.3 Stability and compensation\nThe loop is stable if, at the **gain crossover** frequency (|Aβ| = 1), the phase margin PM = 180° + ∠Aβ is positive; **PM ≥ 45° to 60°** is the usual target (a lower PM gives ringing and overshoot). A two-pole amplifier with A_0 = 10^5, a dominant pole at 10 Hz (GBW = 1 MHz) and β = 1: the crossover is at about 1 MHz, where the first pole contributes -90° and a second pole at 1 MHz another -45°: **PM = 45°**; a second pole at 2 MHz gives PM = 63°. The gain margin is the amount by which |Aβ| < 1 at the frequency where the phase is -180°.\n- **Compensation:** make one pole **dominant** (low frequency) by adding a capacitor (pole splitting, **Miller compensation**: a capacitor C_c across the second gain stage gives an effective capacitance of C_c × (1 + A_2) and a dominant pole near the first stage). Op-amps such as the 741 are internally compensated so that they are stable for unity-gain feedback, at the cost of a low GBW (1 MHz). Stability with a capacitive load needs an isolation resistor.\n- **Gain-bandwidth trade-off** and **slew rate** limit large-signal speed.\n\n## Chapter 2. Operational Amplifier Circuits\n\n### 2.1 The ideal and the real op-amp\nIdeal: infinite gain, bandwidth, input resistance; zero output resistance, offset and noise. Analysis rules for negative feedback: **v+ = v-** (virtual short) and **i+ = i- = 0**.\n**Real limitations:**\n- **Finite gain A and GBW:** closed-loop gain 100 with A = 10^5 has a gain error of 0.1 percent; with a GBW of 1 MHz the bandwidth is 10 kHz.\n- **Slew rate (SR):** the maximum rate of change of the output (for example 0.5 V/μs for a 741). A sine wave of peak V_p at frequency f needs SR ≥ 2π f V_p: the **full-power bandwidth** f_max = SR/(2π V_p) = 0.5 V/μs/(2π × 10 V) = **7.96 kHz** for V_p = 10 V (slew-rate distortion above this).\n- **Input offset voltage V_os** (about 1 to 5 mV for a general-purpose op-amp) is amplified by the noise gain: with V_os = 2 mV and gain 100, the output error is 0.2 V. **Bias currents** I_B (nA to μA) cause offsets across the source resistances: add a resistor R_1 ∥ R_f at the non-inverting input to balance them.\n- **CMRR and PSRR** (finite rejection of common-mode and supply signals), **output swing** (limited by the supply rails), **output current**, **input common-mode range**, and **noise** (en in nV/√Hz).\n- **Rail-to-rail**, **precision**, **low-noise** and **high-speed** op-amps trade these parameters against each other.\n\n### 2.2 Standard circuits\n- **Inverting amplifier:** A_v = -R_f/R_in; R_in(circuit) = R_in. **Non-inverting:** A_v = 1 + R_f/R_g; very high R_in. **Follower:** A_v = 1 (buffer).\n- **Summing amplifier:** v_o = -R_f Σ(v_i/R_i). **Difference amplifier** with R_2/R_1 ratios: v_o = (R_2/R_1)(v_2 - v_1); its CMRR is limited by resistor matching: with 1 percent resistors the CMRR is about 40 dB (the worst case of the ratio error: (1 + gain)/(4 × tolerance)).\n- **Instrumentation amplifier** (two buffers plus a difference amplifier): gain = 1 + 2R/R_g (R = 25 kΩ, R_g = 1 kΩ gives 51); very high input resistance and high CMRR: used for bridges, ECG and strain gauges.\n- **Integrator:** v_o = -(1/RC)∫v_in dt (with a resistor in parallel with C to limit the DC gain); **differentiator:** v_o = -RC dv_in/dt (use a series input resistor for stability). A 10 kΩ and 1 nF integrator has a unity-gain frequency of 1/(2πRC) = 15.9 kHz.\n- **Precision rectifier** (the diode inside the feedback loop removes the 0.7 V drop), **peak detector**, **log and antilog amplifiers** (using diode or BJT exponentials: v_o = -V_T ln(v_in/(I_S R))), **current sources** (V-to-I converters), **voltage references** and **transimpedance amplifiers** (photodiodes: v_o = -I_ph R_f).\n- **Comparators:** an op-amp without feedback compares two voltages; dedicated comparators are faster. **Schmitt trigger** (positive feedback, a hysteresis loop): inverting version with the feedback ratio R_1/(R_1 + R_2): thresholds ±V_sat R_1/(R_1 + R_2) = ±12 V × 10 k/100 k = ±1.2 V, hysteresis 2.4 V: a noisy slow input produces a clean digital edge (switch debouncing, signal conditioning).\n\n## Chapter 3. Active Filters\n\n### 3.1 Filter basics\nA filter passes the wanted band and rejects others: **low-pass, high-pass, band-pass, band-stop (notch)**. Specified by cut-off frequency f_c, order n (the roll-off is 20n dB/decade), and the shape of the response: **Butterworth** (maximally flat magnitude), **Chebyshev** (steeper, with ripple in the passband), **Bessel** (flat group delay, best for pulses), elliptic. The second-order transfer function: H(s) = ω_0²/(s² + (ω_0/Q) s + ω_0²) with Q = 0.707 for a Butterworth (maximally flat) response, Q > 0.707 for a peaking response.\n- **First-order active filters:** an inverting integrator-type low-pass with the corner f_c = 1/(2π R_f C_f) and a gain -R_f/R: with R_f = 100 kΩ, C_f = 1.59 nF, f_c = 1 kHz (see Electronics I).\n\n### 3.2 The Sallen-Key low-pass filter\nA unity-gain Sallen-Key stage: two equal resistors R in series, a capacitor C_2 from the non-inverting input to ground, and a capacitor C_1 from the junction of the resistors back to the op-amp output (a voltage follower). **H(s) = 1/(s² R² C_1 C_2 + 2 s R C_2 + 1)**, so **f_0 = 1/(2π R sqrt(C_1 C_2))** and **Q = (1/2) sqrt(C_1/C_2)**. For a Butterworth response Q = 0.707 requires **C_1 = 2 C_2**. Example: R = 10 kΩ, C_2 = 10 nF, C_1 = 20 nF: f_0 = 1.125 kHz, Q = 0.707: the gain at f_0 is -3 dB (0.707), at 10 f_0 it is -40 dB with a phase of -172°. Cascade stages for higher orders (a fourth-order Butterworth uses two stages with Q = 0.54 and 1.31). **Multiple feedback (MFB)** and **state-variable / biquad** filters give band-pass and notch responses and a high Q.\n- Design hints: use the op-amp's GBW at least 20 to 100 times f_0; use 1 percent resistors and C0G/NP0 capacitors; scale impedances for noise and loading; **switched-capacitor** filters realise resistors with switches and capacitors (IC filters).\n\n## Chapter 4. Differential Amplifiers, Current Mirrors and Multistage Design\n\n- **Differential pair:** two matched transistors sharing a tail current source I_EE. A BJT pair: differential gain A_d = g_m R_C (single-ended output), differential input resistance 2 r_π; the common-mode gain is small when the tail source is a high-impedance current source, so **CMRR = A_d/A_cm** is large (often 80 to 120 dB). The input offset arises from mismatches.\n- **Current mirrors** copy a reference current: output current I_out = I_ref (for matched transistors; the ratio of areas scales it); output resistance r_o; the Wilson and cascode mirrors raise the output resistance. **Active loads** (a current mirror as the load of a differential pair) give a high gain from one stage with a small supply.\n- **A typical op-amp architecture:** the **input differential stage** (high input resistance, rejects common-mode), the **voltage-gain stage** with Miller compensation (high gain, dominant pole), the **output stage** (class AB push-pull buffer, low output resistance). Open-loop gain 10^5 to 10^6, GBW from 1 MHz to GHz.\n- **Cascades:** gains multiply but bandwidth shrinks; each stage must be biased and AC-coupled or directly coupled with level shifting.\n\n## Chapter 5. Oscillators and Timing Circuits\n\n### 5.1 Sinusoidal oscillators\nThe **Barkhausen criterion:** oscillation starts at the frequency where the loop gain Aβ = 1∠0° (the total phase shift is 0° or 360°) and |Aβ| ≥ 1 initially (growing), with a gain-limiting mechanism to stabilise the amplitude.\n- **Wien-bridge oscillator:** an RC series-parallel network (R, C in series and in parallel) with an attenuation of 1/3 at **f_0 = 1/(2π RC)** (R = 10 kΩ and C = 15.9 nF give 1 kHz); the non-inverting amplifier must have a gain of 3 (R_f = 2 R_g); a lamp or a diode network provides the amplitude control. Good for audio.\n- **RC phase-shift oscillator:** three RC sections give 180° at f = 1/(2π RC sqrt 6) and the amplifier must have a gain of at least 29 (inverting).\n- **LC oscillators** (high frequency): **Colpitts** (a capacitive divider), **Hartley** (a tapped inductor): f_0 = 1/(2π sqrt(LC)); **crystal oscillators** (quartz resonator with a very high Q, accuracy 10 to 50 ppm; the Pierce circuit) set the clocks of microcontrollers. **Voltage-controlled oscillators** (VCO) and the **phase-locked loop** (PLL) synthesise frequencies.\n\n### 5.2 Relaxation oscillators and the 555 timer\n- **Astable multivibrator with an op-amp:** an RC integrator and a Schmitt trigger produce a square wave.\n- **The 555 timer:** two comparators, an SR flip-flop and a discharge transistor with thresholds 1/3 and 2/3 V_cc. **Astable:** f = 1.44/((R_1 + 2 R_2) C), duty cycle = (R_1 + R_2)/(R_1 + 2 R_2). With R_1 = 1 kΩ, R_2 = 10 kΩ, C = 0.1 μF: f = 1.44/(21 kΩ × 0.1 μF) = **686 Hz**, duty cycle 11/21 = **52.4 percent**. **Monostable:** the pulse width T = 1.1 R C: R = 100 kΩ, C = 10 μF gives **1.1 s**. Used for delays, flashers, pulse stretching and PWM.\n\n## Chapter 6. Power Amplifiers and Power Supplies\n\n### 6.1 Output stages and classes\n- **Class A:** the transistor conducts for the whole cycle; excellent linearity, poor efficiency: the maximum efficiency is **25 percent** (resistive load) or 50 percent (transformer/inductor coupling). **Class B** (complementary push-pull): each transistor conducts for half the cycle; the maximum efficiency is π/4 = **78.5 percent**, but there is **crossover distortion** because of the V_BE dead zone. **Class AB:** a small bias current (two diode drops) removes the crossover distortion with an efficiency between 50 and 78 percent: the standard for audio. **Class D:** switching (PWM) with a filter at the output: efficiency over 90 percent (audio amplifiers, motor drives).\n- **Example (class B, V_cc = 12 V supplies, 8 Ω load, peak output 12 V):** P_out = V_p²/(2R_L) = 9 W, P_supply = 2 V_cc V_p/(π R_L) = 11.46 W, efficiency 78.5 percent; the **maximum device dissipation** occurs at V_p = 2 V_cc/π = 7.6 V: P_D,max (total of both devices) = 2 V_cc²/(π² R_L) = 3.65 W (not at full output): size the heat sink for it.\n- **Thermal design:** T_J = T_A + P_D (θ_JC + θ_CS + θ_SA); thermal runaway in BJTs is avoided with emitter resistors and thermal tracking of the bias diodes.\n\n### 6.2 Voltage regulators\n- **Linear regulators** (zener reference plus a pass transistor, or IC types 78xx and LDOs): a series pass element dissipates P = (V_in - V_out) I: a 12 V input and 5 V at 0.5 A wastes 3.5 W (efficiency 5/12 = 42 percent). Specifications: **dropout voltage, line and load regulation, ripple rejection, quiescent current**.\n- **Switching regulators:** the **buck (step-down) converter** switches V_in with duty cycle D through an inductor and a capacitor: **V_out = D V_in** (continuous conduction): 12 V to 5 V needs D = 0.417; the inductor ripple current ΔI_L = (V_in - V_out) D/(L f) = 7 × 0.417/(47 μH × 100 kHz) = **0.62 A**; the output ripple voltage ≈ ΔI_L/(8 f C) = 0.62/(8 × 100 kHz × 100 μF) = 7.8 mV. Efficiency 85 to 95 percent. **Boost** (V_out = V_in/(1 - D)), **buck-boost** and **flyback** (isolated). The trade-off: efficiency, size, noise (EMI).\n- **References:** bandgap (about 1.25 V, temperature-stable: PTAT + CTAT).\n\n## Chapter 7. Noise, Precision and Practical Design\n\n- **Noise:** thermal (Johnson) noise voltage v_n = sqrt(4 k T R Δf): a 10 kΩ resistor over a 20 kHz bandwidth at 300 K gives 1.82 μV rms; shot noise, flicker (1/f) noise, the equivalent input noise of op-amps (nV/√Hz) and the **signal-to-noise ratio**. Reduce noise with lower resistor values, a narrower bandwidth, low-noise devices and good shielding and grounding.\n- **Layout and grounding:** decouple every supply pin (100 nF ceramic close to the chip plus bulk capacitance), separate analog and digital grounds joined at one point, short feedback paths, guard rings for high impedances; beware of parasitic capacitance and inductance.\n- **Simulation (SPICE) and measurement:** DC operating point, AC sweep (Bode plot, phase margin), transient (slew, overshoot), noise and Monte Carlo analyses; verify against the hand calculations; measure with a scope using proper probe compensation.\n- **Design flow:** specification → topology choice → hand design with the approximate formulas → simulation including component tolerances → prototype → test → iterate.\n\n## Chapter 8. Common Mistakes Checklist\n1. Applying the ideal op-amp rules without checking for negative feedback (a comparator has none), or forgetting saturation at the supply rails.\n2. Ignoring the GBW: a gain of 100 stage from a 1 MHz GBW op-amp has only a 10 kHz bandwidth.\n3. Forgetting the loading of the feedback network when computing A and β.\n4. Using the closed-loop gain instead of the **noise gain** for offset and stability calculations.\n5. Designing filters with an op-amp whose GBW is too low at the cut-off frequency, or with large resistors that add noise.\n6. Confusing the maximum efficiency (at peak output) with the maximum dissipation (at an intermediate output) in class B stages.\n7. Neglecting phase margin: ringing and oscillation in a \"stable on paper\" design.\n8. Forgetting supply decoupling and the thermal limits of power devices.\n","plan":"# Electronics II (Analog Design): 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 10 to 12 hours per week (3 reading, 6 to 7 design problems and simulation, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Analog design is learned by designing, simulating and measuring. Install LTspice (or ngspice) and build at least one real circuit each week from a handful of op-amps (for example the TL072, LM358, OP07), a 555 timer, resistors, capacitors, transistors and a breadboard.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Adel Sedra and Kenneth Smith, *Microelectronic Circuits* (the feedback, filter, oscillator and output-stage chapters).\n- Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill, *The Art of Electronics*. The practical reference.\n- Sergio Franco, *Design with Operational Amplifiers and Analog Integrated Circuits*.\n- Behzad Razavi, *Fundamentals of Microelectronics* and *Design of Analog CMOS Integrated Circuits* (later).\n- Walt Jung (ed.), *Op Amp Applications Handbook* (free from Analog Devices); Texas Instruments' *Op Amps for Everyone*.\n\n## Free courses and tools\n- MIT OpenCourseWare 6.301/6.071; Analog Devices' ADALM2000 active-learning labs; TI Precision Labs (free videos).\n- LTspice, ngspice, Falstad's circuit simulator; TI's WEBENCH and Analog Devices' filter design tools for active filters and regulators.\n- Application notes from TI, Analog Devices and Maxim on filters, stability and layout.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Feedback fundamentals (Ch 1.1, 1.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1. Verify the gain-bandwidth trade-off in a simulator.\n\n**Week 2: Op-amp circuits and real op-amp limits (Ch 2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q2, Q3. Measure the slew rate of an op-amp on the bench.\n\n**Week 3: Instrumentation amplifiers, integrators, differentiators (Ch 2.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q4, Q5.\n\n**Week 4: Comparators, Schmitt triggers, transimpedance amplifiers (Ch 2.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q6, Q7. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week.\n\n**Week 5: Active filters I: first and second order, Sallen-Key (Ch 3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1. Build and measure a 1 kHz Butterworth filter.\n\n**Week 6: Active filters II: higher order, band-pass, filter types (Ch 3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q2.\n\n**Week 7: Oscillators: Wien-bridge, RC, LC, crystal (Ch 5.1).** Do: Assignment 2 Q3, Q5. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 8: The 555 timer and relaxation oscillators (Ch 5.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q4, Q6.\n\n**Week 9: Stability, phase margin and compensation (Ch 1.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1, Q2. Plot the loop gain and phase margin with an AC simulation.\n\n**Week 10: Output stages and power amplifiers (Ch 6.1).** Do: Assignment 3 Q3. Simulate class B crossover distortion and its cure.\n\n**Week 11: Linear and switching regulators (Ch 6.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q4, Q5. Build a buck converter module and measure its efficiency.\n\n**Week 12: Differential pairs, current mirrors, op-amp internals (Ch 4).** Do: Assignment 3 Q6.\n\n**Week 13: Noise, layout and a design project (Ch 7).** Do: Assignment 3 Q7. Design, simulate and build a small analog front end (sensor amplifier plus filter plus ADC driver).\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, formula sheet only).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can analyse feedback amplifiers: gain, desensitivity, bandwidth, input/output resistance.\n- [ ] I can design and analyse op-amp circuits and account for offset, bias, GBW and slew rate.\n- [ ] I can design second-order active filters (Sallen-Key) to a specification.\n- [ ] I can design Wien-bridge, LC and 555-based oscillators and timers.\n- [ ] I can determine the stability of a feedback amplifier from the phase margin and compensate it.\n- [ ] I can analyse class A, B and AB output stages (efficiency, dissipation, distortion).\n- [ ] I can design linear and buck regulators with thermal and ripple calculations.\n- [ ] I can explain differential pairs and current mirrors and estimate their gain and errors.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with a formula sheet only.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. For every design, calculate by hand, then simulate (including tolerances), then build if you can.\n2. Always check the op-amp's datasheet limits (GBW, slew rate, supply, output swing, input common-mode range).\n3. Ask \"what could go wrong?\" (saturation, oscillation, thermal limits) before finishing a design.\n4. Keep a catalogue of building blocks with their gain formulas and input/output resistances.\n5. Read application notes: practical analog design lives in them.\n","test":"# Electronics II (Analog Design): Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Assume ideal op-amps unless stated.\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** For a feedback amplifier with a large loop gain Aβ, the closed-loop gain is approximately\nA) A  B) 1/β  C) Aβ  D) A + β\n\n**2.** Negative feedback does all of the following except\nA) it reduces gain sensitivity  B) it extends bandwidth  C) it reduces distortion  D) it increases the open-loop gain\n\n**3.** The gain-bandwidth product of a single-pole op-amp is\nA) proportional to the gain  B) constant  C) zero  D) infinite\n\n**4.** An inverting amplifier with R_f = 100 kΩ and R_in = 10 kΩ has a gain of\nA) +10  B) -10  C) +11  D) -11\n\n**5.** A non-inverting amplifier with R_f = 20 kΩ and R_g = 10 kΩ has a gain of\nA) 2  B) 3  C) -2  D) -3\n\n**6.** The \"virtual short\" in an ideal op-amp with negative feedback means\nA) v+ = v-  B) the output is shorted  C) the input current is large  D) the supply is shorted\n\n**7.** The slew rate of an op-amp limits\nA) the input resistance  B) the maximum rate of change of the output voltage  C) the supply current  D) the offset voltage\n\n**8.** Input offset voltage in an amplifier is multiplied at the output by\nA) 1  B) the noise gain  C) the slew rate  D) zero\n\n**9.** An op-amp integrator's output for a constant positive input is\nA) a constant  B) a negative ramp  C) a sine wave  D) zero\n\n**10.** A Schmitt trigger uses\nA) negative feedback  B) positive feedback giving hysteresis  C) no feedback  D) a capacitor only\n\n**11.** An instrumentation amplifier is chosen for\nA) high input resistance and high CMRR  B) high-power output  C) high-frequency generation  D) low gain only\n\n**12.** A second-order Butterworth low-pass filter has Q equal to\nA) 0.5  B) 0.707  C) 1  D) 10\n\n**13.** A fourth-order low-pass filter has a roll-off of\nA) 20 dB/decade  B) 40 dB/decade  C) 60 dB/decade  D) 80 dB/decade\n\n**14.** The Wien-bridge oscillator frequency is\nA) 1/(2π RC)  B) 1/(2π sqrt(LC))  C) 1.44/(RC)  D) 2π RC\n\n**15.** The Wien-bridge amplifier gain at oscillation must be\nA) 1  B) 3  C) 29  D) 100\n\n**16.** A 555 timer in monostable mode has a pulse width of\nA) 0.693 RC  B) 1.1 RC  C) 1.44/(RC)  D) RC/2\n\n**17.** The maximum theoretical efficiency of a class B output stage is\nA) 25 percent  B) 50 percent  C) 78.5 percent  D) 100 percent\n\n**18.** Crossover distortion occurs in\nA) class A  B) class B  C) class D only  D) linear regulators\n\n**19.** A buck converter with D = 0.5 and V_in = 12 V has an output of about\nA) 24 V  B) 12 V  C) 6 V  D) 3 V\n\n**20.** A phase margin of 45° in a feedback amplifier gives\nA) a stable but ringing step response  B) instability  C) no overshoot at all  D) infinite gain\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (10 marks)** An amplifier has A = 2000 and the feedback network gives β = 0.05. (a) Find the closed-loop gain, the ideal gain 1/β, and the percentage error [3]. (b) Find (1 + Aβ) and the effect on the output resistance if R_out(open loop) = 1 kΩ (voltage sampling) [3]. (c) If A drops by 10 percent, estimate the change in A_f [2]. (d) If the amplifier's GBW is 2 MHz, find the bandwidth with feedback assuming a single pole [2].\n\n**B2. (10 marks)** (a) A summing inverting amplifier has R_f = 47 kΩ, R_1 = 10 kΩ (v_1 = 0.5 V) and R_2 = 22 kΩ (v_2 = -1 V): find v_o [3]. (b) An instrumentation amplifier has 10 kΩ feedback resistors and R_g = 200 Ω in the standard three-op-amp circuit (gain 1 + 2R/R_g): find the gain [2]. (c) A 10 V peak, 10 kHz sine wave is required from an op-amp: find the minimum slew rate [3]. (d) An integrator with R = 10 kΩ and C = 1 nF: find the unity-gain frequency [2].\n\n**B3. (10 marks)** (a) Find the frequency of a Wien-bridge oscillator with R = 16 kΩ and C = 10 nF [2]. (b) Design R_f and R_g for the required amplifier gain [2]. (c) For a 555 astable with R_1 = 1 kΩ, R_2 = 10 kΩ and C = 0.1 μF find the frequency and the duty cycle [4]. (d) An inverting Schmitt trigger with R_1 = 10 kΩ and R_2 = 22 kΩ in the positive feedback path and ±5 V output: find the thresholds [2].\n\n**B4. (10 marks)** A class B stage with ±15 V supplies drives an 8 Ω load to a peak of 15 V. (a) Find P_out, P_supply and the efficiency [4]. (b) Find the total transistor dissipation [2]. (c) A 7809 regulator supplies 9 V at 0.2 A from 15 V: find the power loss and the efficiency [2]. (d) A buck converter delivers 5 V from 12 V: find the duty cycle [2].\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **B** · 2 **D** · 3 **B** · 4 **B** · 5 **B** · 6 **A** · 7 **B** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **B** · 11 **A** · 12 **B** · 13 **D** · 14 **A** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **C** · 18 **B** · 19 **C** · 20 **A**\n\nBrief reasons: 2. Feedback reduces the gain; it does not increase A. 13. 20 dB/decade per order: 4 × 20. 19. V_out = D V_in = 6 V.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Step 1: A = 2000 and β = 0.05 (from the question), so Aβ = 100.\nStep 2: A_f = A/(1 + Aβ) = 2000/(1 + 100) = **19.8**.\nStep 3: the ideal gain 1/β = **20**; the error = (20 - 19.8)/20 = **0.99 percent** (1/(1 + Aβ)).\n(b) Step 1: 1 + Aβ = 1 + 100 = **101**.\nStep 2: voltage-sampling feedback reduces the output resistance by this factor: R_out,f = 1 kΩ/101 = **9.9 Ω**.\n(c) The closed-loop gain changes by (dA/A)/(1 + Aβ) = 10 percent/101 = **0.1 percent**.\n(d) BW_f = GBW/A_f = 2 MHz/19.8 = **101 kHz** (equivalently f_H (1 + Aβ)).\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Step 1: v_o = -R_f (v_1/R_1 + v_2/R_2) = -47 kΩ (0.5/10 kΩ + (-1)/22 kΩ).\nStep 2: = -47 (0.05 - 0.04545) = -47 × 0.00455 = **-0.214 V**.\n(b) Gain = 1 + 2R/R_g = 1 + 2 × 10 kΩ/200 Ω = 1 + 100 = **101**.\n(c) SR ≥ 2π f V_p = 2π × 10^4 × 10 = 6.28 × 10^5 V/s = **0.628 V/μs** (use an op-amp with at least 1 V/μs).\n(d) f_unity = 1/(2π R C) = 1/(2π × 10^4 × 10^-9) = **15.9 kHz**.\n\n**B3.**\n(a) f = 1/(2π RC) = 1/(2π × 16 kΩ × 10 nF) = 1/(1.005 × 10^-3) = **995 Hz**.\n(b) The gain must be 3: 1 + R_f/R_g = 3, so **R_f = 2 R_g** (for example R_g = 10 kΩ, R_f = 20 kΩ plus an amplitude-control element).\n(c) Step 1: f = 1.44/((R_1 + 2R_2) C) = 1.44/(21 kΩ × 0.1 μF) = 1.44/(2.1 × 10^-3) = **686 Hz**.\nStep 2: duty cycle = (R_1 + R_2)/(R_1 + 2R_2) = 11/21 = **52.4 percent**.\n(d) Step 1: thresholds = ±V_sat R_1/(R_1 + R_2) = ±5 × 10/(10 + 22) = ±5 × 0.3125 = **±1.56 V**.\nStep 2: hysteresis = 2 × 1.56 = 3.1 V. (Using R_1 = 10 kΩ to ground and R_2 = 22 kΩ as the feedback resistor.)\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Step 1: P_out = V_p²/(2R_L) = 225/16 = **14.06 W**.\nStep 2: P_supply = 2 V_cc V_p/(π R_L) = 2 × 15 × 15/(π × 8) = 450/25.13 = **17.9 W**.\nStep 3: efficiency = 14.06/17.9 = **78.5 percent**.\n(b) P_D = P_supply - P_out = 17.9 - 14.06 = **3.84 W** (about 1.9 W per transistor).\n(c) Step 1: P_loss = (V_in - V_out) I = (15 - 9) × 0.2 = **1.2 W**.\nStep 2: efficiency = V_out/V_in = 9/15 = **60 percent**.\n(d) D = V_out/V_in = 5/12 = **0.417**.\n"},mcq:[{"q":"For a feedback amplifier with a large loop gain Aβ, the closed-loop gain is approximately","options":["A","1/β","Aβ","A + β"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Negative feedback does all of the following except","options":["it reduces gain sensitivity","it extends bandwidth","it reduces distortion","it increases the open-loop gain"],"answer":3,"explain":"Feedback reduces the gain; it does not increase A.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The gain-bandwidth product of a single-pole op-amp is","options":["proportional to the gain","constant","zero","infinite"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An inverting amplifier with R_f = 100 kΩ and R_in = 10 kΩ has a gain of","options":["+10","-10","+11","-11"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A non-inverting amplifier with R_f = 20 kΩ and R_g = 10 kΩ has a gain of","options":["2","3","-2","-3"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The \"virtual short\" in an ideal op-amp with negative feedback means","options":["v+ = v-","the output is shorted","the input current is large","the supply is shorted"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The slew rate of an op-amp limits","options":["the input resistance","the maximum rate of change of the output voltage","the supply current","the offset voltage"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Input offset voltage in an amplifier is multiplied at the output by","options":["1","the noise gain","the slew rate","zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An op-amp integrator's output for a constant positive input is","options":["a constant","a negative ramp","a sine wave","zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A Schmitt trigger uses","options":["negative feedback","positive feedback giving hysteresis","no feedback","a capacitor only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An instrumentation amplifier is chosen for","options":["high input resistance and high CMRR","high-power output","high-frequency generation","low gain only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A second-order Butterworth low-pass filter has Q equal to","options":["0.5","0.707","1","10"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A fourth-order low-pass filter has a roll-off of","options":["20 dB/decade","40 dB/decade","60 dB/decade","80 dB/decade"],"answer":3,"explain":"20 dB/decade per order: 4 ×","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Wien-bridge oscillator frequency is","options":["1/(2π RC)","1/(2π sqrt(LC))","1.44/(RC)","2π RC"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Wien-bridge amplifier gain at oscillation must be","options":["1","3","29","100"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A 555 timer in monostable mode has a pulse width of","options":["0.693 RC","1.1 RC","1.44/(RC)","RC/2"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The maximum theoretical efficiency of a class B output stage is","options":["25 percent","50 percent","78.5 percent","100 percent"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Crossover distortion occurs in","options":["class A","class B","class D only","linear regulators"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A buck converter with D = 0.5 and V_in = 12 V has an output of about","options":["24 V","12 V","6 V","3 V"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A phase margin of 45° in a feedback amplifier gives","options":["a stable but ringing step response","instability","no overshoot at all","infinite gain"],"answer":0,"explain":"19. V_out = D V_in = 6 V.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The closed-loop gain of a feedback amplifier is","options":["A(1 + Aβ)","A/(1 + Aβ)","Aβ","A - β"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Series mixing with shunt sampling at the output (series-shunt feedback) gives","options":["low R_in and high R_out","high R_in and low R_out","low R_in and low R_out","high R_in and high R_out"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The noise gain of a non-inverting amplifier with R_f = 99 kΩ and R_g = 1 kΩ is","options":["1","99","100","0.01"],"answer":2,"explain":"Noise gain = 1 + R_f/R_g = 100.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A 1 MHz GBW op-amp with a gain of 50 has a small-signal bandwidth of","options":["20 kHz","50 kHz","1 MHz","50 MHz"],"answer":0,"explain":"1 MHz/50 = 20 kHz.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Slew-rate limiting distorts","options":["small signals at low frequencies","large, high-frequency signals","DC signals","only square waves of low amplitude"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The bias current compensation resistor in an inverting amplifier equals","options":["R_f","R_in","R_in ∥ R_f","0"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"An op-amp comparator without feedback","options":["has a virtual short","saturates according to the sign of v+ - v-","amplifies linearly","oscillates"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The thresholds of an inverting Schmitt trigger depend on","options":["the supply only","the saturation voltage and the feedback ratio","the input frequency","the load"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A unity-gain Sallen-Key low-pass with equal resistors has Q = 0.707 when","options":["C_1 = C_2","C_1 = 2 C_2","C_1 = C_2/2","R_1 = 2 R_2"],"answer":1,"explain":"Q = (1/2) sqrt(C_1/C_2).","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Each additional pole in a low-pass filter adds roll-off of","options":["6 dB/octave","20 dB/decade","both of the above (they are equal)","60 dB/decade"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Barkhausen criterion requires, at the oscillation frequency, a loop phase shift of","options":["90°","180°","0° or 360°","45°"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The 555 astable frequency is","options":["1.1/(RC)","1.44/((R_1 + 2R_2)","C) 0.693 RC","1/(2π RC)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A crystal oscillator is preferred in a microcontroller because of its","options":["low cost only","high Q and frequency stability","large size","wide tolerance"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The phase margin is the phase of the loop gain relative to -180° at the frequency where","options":["|Aβ| = 1","the phase is zero","|Aβ| = 0","the gain is maximum"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Miller compensation makes the amplifier stable by","options":["adding a zero","creating a dominant low-frequency pole and pushing the second pole higher","removing feedback","increasing the supply"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In class AB output stages the quiescent current is set to","options":["zero","a small value that removes the crossover distortion","the maximum rating","the load current"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The maximum efficiency of a class A stage with a resistive load is","options":["25 percent","50 percent","78.5 percent","100 percent"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Compared with a linear regulator for a large V_in - V_out, a switching regulator has","options":["lower efficiency","higher efficiency but more noise","lower cost always","no ripple"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a buck converter V_out = D V_in. For V_in = 15 V and V_out = 5 V the duty cycle is","options":["0.25","0.33","0.5","0.75"],"answer":1,"explain":"5/15 = 0.33.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Thermal (Johnson) noise voltage increases with","options":["the square root of resistance and bandwidth","1/R","frequency only","the supply voltage"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
